---
/* Text on a path — the sentence surfaces word by word along a curve that is
   never drawn.

   Cloned from annnimate.com/animations/text-on-path. Their page is a shell; the
   live preview runs in an iframe (/api/sandbox/iframe/text-on-path) that ships
   the working component, so the geometry here is the measured original and not
   an impression of it — the curve, the 1600x900 design space, the radial
   offsets, the curvature correction, the 0.6s blur bloom. The full extraction is
   in docs/research/components/text-on-path.spec.md.

   What is deliberately not theirs: the runtime. The original drives this with
   GSAP + ScrollTrigger; text-on-path.js does the same work on a plain rAF loop,
   because nothing else on this site ships an animation library and one section
   is not worth 110 KB.

   The element is empty on purpose — the script measures every glyph and writes
   the SVG. The paragraph below it is what a reader without JS gets: the same
   sentence, flat and legible — and it is the copy a crawler reads, since the
   script only swaps it out once JS runs.

   The sentence itself is the positioning claim, not a mood line: this is the
   first thing after the hero, so it has to say what the work actually is —
   user research and interface craft, in that order, from one pair of hands.
   Two beats to match the curve's rise and fall, and ~100 characters because
   the layout justifies by stretching word gaps only (see text-on-path.js);
   much longer and the tail runs off the end of the path.

   The pivot between the two clauses is an asterisk, set as a plain character in
   the run — not the drawn mark this once carried. That version built the glyph
   itself (three bars at 60 degrees, its own reveal turn, later a raymarched
   glass pass) and it is gone on purpose: the beat only ever needed to be a
   typographic one, and a real '*' gets measured, spaced and revealed by exactly
   the same code as every other glyph on the curve. */
const TEXT =
  'User research finds the real problem * interface craft makes the answer obvious. I do both, in order.';

/* Authored in a 1600x900 space and overshooting both edges (x runs -20 to 1620)
   so the line still reads edge to edge once the viewBox is sliced to the
   viewport's aspect ratio. */
const PATH =
  'M -20 780 C 80 760, 150 690, 210 580 C 275 460, 350 395, 460 385 C 570 375, 640 415, 720 455 C 800 495, 870 515, 950 495 C 1030 475, 1060 400, 1110 310 C 1155 228, 1210 185, 1290 182 C 1370 180, 1420 250, 1460 350 C 1505 465, 1545 590, 1620 680';
---
<section
  class="tpath"
  data-text-on-path
  data-text={TEXT}
  data-path={PATH}
  data-text-position="above"
  data-split="words"
  data-scrub-distance="300"
  data-direction="ltr"
  data-font-size="48"
  data-letter-spacing="0em"
  data-curve-spread="1"
  data-disable=""
>
  <p class="tpath-flat">{TEXT}</p>
</section>
